## Work inside a project

Open a file beneath a generated project's root, or change Neovim's working directory to it. Rivet finds an owning `.rivet.json` or the nearest supported native project marker, and remembers a project created during the session. Existing native projects do not need conversion. Outset-generated projects use the migration described below. See [Existing projects](/docs/existing-projects/).

| Command | Action |
| --- | --- |
| `:RivetFile` | Preview and add a source, header, pair, class, or module unit. |
| `:RivetFiles` | Review a registered C/C++ file move, rename, or removal. |
| `:RivetModuleRename` | Review a C++ module identity change and registered imports. |
| `:RivetRepair` | Back up an edited managed file and restore its generated snapshot. |
| `:RivetRunOptions` | Review runtime arguments, environment, parallel settings, and hooks. |
| `:RivetHeader` | Review an addition, edit, or removal of the current file's comment header. |
| `:RivetHeaderDefaults` | Review header defaults for future project files. |
| `:RivetTarget` | Add a provider-supported application/library; see [Files and targets](/docs/templates/). |
| `:RivetDependency` | C++: register a pinned CPM package and its target linkage. |
| `:RivetCMake` | Inspect and edit the selected build's CMake cache. |
| `:RivetTask` | Choose a task; choose a preset the first time. |
| `:RivetPreset` | Change the remembered CMake preset or native Debug/Release choice. |
| `:RivetBuildPreset` / `:RivetTestPreset` | CMake: choose associated build/test presets. |
| `:RivetBuildTarget` | CMake: choose a Build target, or use preset defaults. |
| `:RivetStatus` | Show active presets and targets. |
| `:RivetProfile` | Review and save reusable project choices. |
| `:RivetIdentity` | Review and save optional local header details. |
| `:RivetDoctor` | Inspect the selected provider's tools; [coverage varies by provider](/docs/troubleshooting/#what-doctor-checks). |
| `:RivetRunTarget` | Change the remembered application target. |
| `:RivetTask build` | Native build; Python checks syntax, CMake configures first. |
| `:RivetTask run` | Run the application through the provider's native workflow. |
| `:RivetTask test` | Run the provider's test workflow. |
| `:RivetTask repeat` | Repeat the previous task using current build choices. |

For CMake, Build configures first and Test/Run configure and build first. Other providers sequence native tools; a failed or stopped step ends the workflow. Run prompts for an application when several exist. Recorded starter test executables are excluded; an existing CMake project's File API products can include test programs, so select the intended application. Preset, application, and last task are remembered per project across editor restarts in Neovim's local state directory.

Output streams into a reused window: Ctrl-C stops the local task's process group, including compiler/application children, and `q` closes the output without stopping the task. [Runtime options](/docs/task-options/#slurm-examples) explains remote cancellation limits. C/C++, Swift, Cargo primary-span diagnostics, and Python traceback locations appear in quickfix; use `:copen`, Enter, `:cnext`, and `:cprevious` to navigate them. Save edited sources before building.

## Keep your language tools

For C++ projects, Rivet generates `.clangd`, `.clang-format`, and `.clang-tidy`. It does not replace your LSP client, completion plugin, debugger, or formatter integration. After configure, `.cache/rivet/compile_commands.json` follows the selected build. Run through Rivet to refresh it, or configure your own `.clangd` database path when using CMake directly.

For missing module diagnostics, follow [Troubleshooting](/docs/troubleshooting/). For cache controls and preset behavior, see [CMake and tasks](/docs/cmake/).

## Language and workload guides

Use [C interoperability](/docs/c/), [Python](/docs/python/), [Rust](/docs/rust/), or [SwiftPM](/docs/swift/) for provider tasks. C++ also offers [HPC](/docs/hpc/) and [graphics](/docs/graphics/) profiles. All providers keep the same review/cancel flow.

## Existing Outset setup

Use `require('rivet').setup(...)` and `:Rivet…` commands in new configuration. The old `require('outset')` entry point still loads Rivet, reports a deprecation through `:checkhealth vim.deprecated`, and will be removed in 0.3.0. Open a project with `.outset.json` and run `:RivetMigrate` to review its conversion and backups before applying. Reconfigure afterward.
